# The repository is LF everywhere — `.editorconfig` sets `end_of_line = lf`.
#
# Git for Windows offers `core.autocrlf=true` as the installer default, which
# checks files out with CRLF. That silently breaks the files a shell interprets:
#
#   .husky/pre-commit, .husky/commit-msg  → `sh` chokes on the trailing \r, so
#       hooks fail with "command not found" for a pnpm that IS on PATH
#   scripts/deploy/helm-deploy.sh,
#   scripts/deploy/oci-bastion-tunnel.sh  → run on Linux VMs; CRLF yields
#       "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M"
#
# Pinning it here means a Windows clone behaves like a Linux one regardless of
# each developer's local `core.autocrlf`. The tree is already pure LF, so this
# introduces no content change — it only prevents future drift.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executables interpreted by a shell: belt and braces on top of the rule above.
*.sh text eol=lf
.husky/* text eol=lf

# Binary assets: never normalise line endings, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico binary
*.svg text eol=lf
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.7z binary
*.wasm binary

# The lockfile is generated: marked so GitHub collapses it in diffs and does
# not count it toward language stats. Deliberately NOT `merge=binary` — that
# would suppress the diff and refuse to auto-merge, which helps nobody. On a
# lockfile conflict, take either side and re-run `pnpm install`.
pnpm-lock.yaml text eol=lf linguist-generated=true
